<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Morocco</h1><p class="st-meta">1415 CE – 1769 CE · capital Ceuta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's chain of fortified enclaves on the Moroccan coast, from Ceuta to Mazagan, the first European overseas expansion of the Age of Discovery.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate</a> in 1415, launching its overseas expansion. Afonso V added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ksar-es-seghir">Ksar es-Seghir</a> in 1458 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asilah">Arzila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 1471. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I</a> the Portuguese built or seized Atlantic posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-morocco">Safi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azemmour">Azemmour</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-jadida">Mazagan</a> between 1505 and 1514.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The enclaves were garrison towns governed by captains, supplied by sea and living from raids, tribute from nearby tribes and trade in grain, horses and textiles, partly for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> trade. Portuguese kings styled themselves lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> beyond the sea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Life was precarious, with constant warfare against Wattasid and Saadi forces.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> in 1541, Portugal abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-morocco">Safi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azemmour">Azemmour</a> and other posts. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian</a>'s disastrous invasion ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcacer-quibir">Alcácer Quibir</a> in 1578. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after 1640, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1661, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-jadida">Mazagan</a> was evacuated in 1769.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1415 CE – 1769 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.86%;width:7.04%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1415 CE – 1769 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ceuta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Afonso V, Manuel I, Sebastian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese North Africa, Algarve d'Além-Mar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=portuguese-morocco" aria-label="Portuguese Morocco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4546 882 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Morocco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4866 1138L4773 1045